  "result": "I wrote the revised record to `record.jsonl`. Its rule logic is unchanged; I only changed the `blind` scenario, which the gate rejected. It has not been through the gate again yet.\n\n**Why `blind` failed:** the scenario masked `rel.surface_gap`, but Core recomputes that gap from the two vehicles' footprints whenever no measured gap is supplied. The admission check requires those footprints for any vehicle whose occupancy is read, and the rule reads occupancy for both vehicles. So hiding the measured gap left the computed one in place, and the result was still \"violated\", as the coder found.\n\n**The fix:** `blind` now masks `kin.speed` instead, in the same scene as `forbid`. With speed hidden, two of the rule's checks become unknown:\n- whether the other vehicle is moving;\n- the required gap, which is its speed times the merge headway. The 20 m gap stays observed, but the 60 m it is compared against becomes unknown.\n\nThe closest-approach check uses velocities, so it still holds. The overall verdict should therefore come out unknown, with `kin.speed` as the only reason.\n\n**Risk:** this assumes Core does not also recompute `kin.speed` from velocity when speed is masked. Its note about recomputing only mentions relations, but this hasn't been tested: the coder's trial with both atoms masked was rejected before evaluation because its mask didn't match the plan. If the gate reports `blind` as decided again, that fallback is the cause.\n\n**Other edits:**\n- The obligation text now says outright that the region is selected again, and every condition on it is written out.\n- The justifications quote the definitions they rely on.\n- I dropped the three parameter proposals, because those parameters are already in the catalogue, and the record names them as they are.",
